<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="methylsalicylate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY18C3-22" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>The organic volatile |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| is one of the more than 400 identified volatile organic compounds (VOCs) in plants detected so far |CITS: [9881368]| which play important roles in defining taste and aroma in fruits such as tomato |CITS:[24692651]|. But VOCs also represent compounds involved in plant defense through luring predators to plants preyed on by pests |CITS:[23955880] [26197563] [16222805]| and reproductive biology by attracting pollinators |CITS:[11960742] [20462477] [23383981] [12921438]|. Although hundreds of VOCs have been found in tomato, |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| belongs to the few aroma and flavor forming volatile compounds which are above the range of olfactory threshold perceivable by humans |CITS:[Buttery87] [Buttery93]|. |FRAME:CPD-6442 Methylsalicylate| is the principal compound (98%) found in essential oil of the perennial plant wintergreen (&lt;i&gt;Gaultheria procumbens&lt;/i&gt;) |CITS:[Middleton93]| and has been frequently used as food and medicinal additive claimed to have contributed to a drop in cardiovascular mortality in the United States |CITS:[Janssen96]|. On the other side, |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| has also been under investigation because of potential poisoning effects on humans |CITS:[8880210]|. The catalyzing enzyme, i.e. salicylate 1-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase has been first characterized from &lt;i&gt;Clarkia breweri&lt;/i&gt; |CITS:[Ross99]| which is also a member of a new methyltransferase family referred to as SABATH |CITS: [DAuria03]|. |FRAME:CPD-110 Salicylate| is known as an hormone that is involved in systemic disease resistant in plants, also referred to as systemic acquired resistance (SAR) |CITS:[17810266] [15283665] [17904410]| but growing evidence points to |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| as the transmitted signal |CITS:[17916738] [17916698] [19131332] [21311035]|. However, in order to initiate SAR |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| must be converted back to |FRAME:CPD-110 salicylate| at the target location because in many cases &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methylester of plant hormones have been shown to be ineffective (see |FRAME:PWY18C3-24 methylsalicylate degradation|) |CITS:[Schulaev97]|. In tomato, a cDNA encoding a salicylate &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase (SAMT1) has been identified, cloned and expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; and subsequently subjected to biochemical assays to determine catalytic properties of the enzyme |CITS:[20070566]|. It could be demonstrated that the SAMT1 encoded protein was very specific for |FRAME:CPD-110 salicylate| forming |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate|, hence acting as a salicylate 1-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-methyltransferase. This finding was confirmed by &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; experiments with sense and antisense transgenic plants either producing significantly more |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| than control plants or significantly less of the |FRAME:CPD-6442 methylsalicylate| product |CITS:[20070566]|. In addition, silencing SAMT1 resulted in a higher infection rate by spider mites (&lt;i&gt;Tetranychus urticae&lt;/i&gt;) in tomato because this plant pest became virtually undetectable for their predators. The switching-off of SAMT1 also made tomato plants more prone towards the root fungus &lt;i&gt;Fusarium oxysporum&lt;/i&gt; indicating that SAMT1 and its encoded enzyme is able to interfere with direct and indirect plant defense reactions |CITS:[20059742]|.</Description>
