    <DataNode elementId="AT5G55250-MONOMER" textLabel="indole-3-acetate carboxyl methyltransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9FLN8" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">IAMT1 is capable of methylating indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [12897246][16169896]|. Methylation fundamentally affects the biochemical properties of this major phytohormone by changing it from an acidic compound to a nonpolar compound. The biological function of methyl-IAA (MeIAA) is still under investigation, but, low levels of this compound can be detected in Arabidopsis tissue |CITS: [18367217]|. In addition, overexpression of the IAMT1 enzyme can cause several auxin-related phenotypes including leaf curvature and altered gravitropism |CITS: [16169896]|. IAMT1 transcript expression varies across tissues and throughout development. There is a notably high level of expression in leaf margins with correlates well with the phenotypes observed in plants with altered levels of IAMT1|CITS: [16169896]|. IAMT1 is a member of the SABATH family of methyltransferases |CITS: [DAURIA03]|. Purified recombinant AtIAMT1 has a kcat of 0.092 s&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; with |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN| and a calculated catalytic efficiency of 2200 s&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; M&lt;sup&gt;-1&lt;/sup&gt; (Kcat/Km) |CITS:[12897246]|. Structural analysis has identified the |FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| binding pocket of the SAM-salicylic acid methyltransferase member of the SABATH family from |FRAME: TAX-36903| |CITS:[12897246]|, and AtIAMT1 shares all of the conserved amino acids in this pocket |CITS:[17499822]|. However, the tryptophan226 residue in the salicyclic acid binding pocket is replaced by a glycine in both PtIAMT1 and AtIAMT1, suggesting that this residue is important for determining substrate specificity |CITS:[12897246 ][17499822]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G55250-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT5G55250.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9FLN8" />
